---
// Paper Fig. "Explicit guidance results", inlined so its prose text follows the
// site theme. Numbers in the notes come from the paper's result macros.
import svg from "@/assets/figs/library-design-bench/mechanism.svg?raw";
---

<figure class="ldb-guidance not-prose">
  <div
    class="mc-chart"
    role="img"
    aria-label="Left: score gain per language from explicit agent-first guidance, split into simplicity and correctness. Right: clirs examples under the standard and explicit-guidance prompts."
    set:html={svg}
  />
  <figcaption>
    <b>Designing explicitly for agents helps, modestly.</b> Left: score gain per
    language when GPT-6 Astra designs with explicit agent-first guidance, split into
    simplicity and correctness. Right: <code>clirs</code> examples under each prompt.
  </figcaption>
  <details class="mc-notes">
    <summary>Setup and results</summary>
    <p>
      <b>Setup.</b> GPT-6 Astra (Codex, high reasoning) designs each library with
      and without a guidance prompt appended to the unchanged specification. The
      prompt says only coding agents, scored on how little code they write, will
      use the library, and asks for consumer-first API sketches, runnable usage examples,
      and testing with subagents. We test the combined intervention, not each piece.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Results.</b> The score rises from 44.1 to 46.4, just below the production
      library (46.6) and standard-prompt Opus 5.5 (48.9). It improves for all three
      implementers and three of four languages, mostly through shorter solutions:
      simplicity contributes +2.5 points and correctness −0.9. Guided libraries also
      look less like the production library, reproducing 13.4% of its exported names
      versus 19.4%; in <code>clirs</code>, builder chains become single
      task-level calls.
    </p>
    <p>
      <b>Cost.</b> Guidance nearly doubles the cost of designing a library ($8.24
      vs. $4.51) and leaves the cost per problem unchanged.
    </p>
  </details>
</figure>

<style is:global>
  .ldb-guidance {
    --mc-ink: var(--foreground);
    --mc-ink-soft: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 62%, transparent);
    --mc-rule: color-mix(in srgb, var(--foreground) 14%, transparent);

    margin: 2rem 0;
    color: var(--foreground);
    font-size: 14px;
  }
  /* Below ~560px the figure scrolls sideways instead of shrinking its text. */
  .mc-chart {
    max-width: 720px;
    margin: 0 auto;
    overflow-x: auto;
  }
  .mc-chart svg {
    display: block;
    width: 100%;
    min-width: 540px;
    height: auto;
  }
  /* Scoped copy of the rule matplotlib ships as a global <style>. */
  .mc-chart svg * {
    stroke-linejoin: round;
    stroke-linecap: butt;
  }
  /* The Rust logo is solid black; flip it so it shows on the dark page. */
  html[data-theme="dark"] .ldb-guidance #mc-AnnotationBbox_1 image {
    filter: invert(1);
  }

  .ldb-guidance figcaption {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    color: var(--mc-ink-soft);
  }
  .ldb-guidance figcaption b,
  .ldb-guidance figcaption code,
  .mc-notes b,
  .mc-notes code {
    color: var(--foreground);
  }

  .mc-notes {
    margin: 16px 0 0;
  }
  .mc-notes p {
    margin: 12px 0 0;
    color: var(--mc-ink-soft);
  }
</style>
